Kept this version direct. For every zero, check all cells that contain one and keep the smallest Manhattan distance. It is slower than a grid BFS, but the calculation is easy to follow and does not depend on queue ordering.

A zero next to a one gets distance `1`, while a cell containing one gets `0` immediately. The empty grid check is included for safety.

```python
class Solution:
    def nearest(self, grid):
        if not grid or not grid[0]:
            return grid

        rows = len(grid)
        cols = len(grid[0])
        ones = []

        for r in range(rows):
            for c in range(cols):
                if grid[r][c] == 1:
                    ones.append((r, c))

        result = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]

        for r in range(rows):
            for c in range(cols):
                if grid[r][c] == 1:
                    continue

                best = rows + cols
                for one_r, one_c in ones:
                    distance = abs(r - one_r) + abs(c - one_c)
                    best = min(best, distance)

                result[r][c] = best

        return result
```

For a grid with `R * C` cells, the worst-case time is `O((R * C)^2)`. Storing the one positions and the result uses `O(R * C)` space.

Dry run: for `[[0, 1], [0, 0]]`, the one is at `(0, 1)`. The other cells receive distances `1`, `2`, and `1`, so the result is `[[1, 0], [2, 1]]`.

This version starts with zero for the cells that already contain one and a large number everywhere else. It repeatedly checks neighboring cells and lowers a value whenever a shorter route is found.

The loop ends when a full pass makes no change. Each update moves through an edge of the grid, so all cells eventually settle on their nearest distance. It is easier to see than a queue, but it can need many passes on a long grid.

```csharp
class Solution {
    public int[][] nearest(int[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.Length == 0 || grid[0].Length == 0) {
            return grid;
        }

        int rows = grid.Length;
        int cols = grid[0].Length;
        int large = rows + cols + 1;
        int[][] distance = new int[rows][];

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            distance[r] = new int[cols];
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                distance[r][c] = grid[r][c] == 1 ? 0 : large;
            }
        }

        bool changed = true;
        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (changed) {
            changed = false;

            for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
                for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                    for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                        int nr = r + dr[k];
                        int nc = c + dc[k];

                        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&
                            distance[nr][nc] + 1 < distance[r][c]) {
                            distance[r][c] = distance[nr][nc] + 1;
                            changed = true;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
}
```

The space use is `O(rows * cols)`. The time depends on the grid diameter and is `O(rows * cols * (rows + cols))` in the worst case. The original array remains unchanged.

Dry run: in a single row `[1, 0, 0]`, the first pass changes the middle value to `1`. A later check changes the last value to `2`, giving `[0, 1, 2]`.

A queue works well here because all moves cost one. First, put every cell containing `1` into the queue with distance `0`. Then expand outward and fill each unvisited cell with the distance from the closest source.

Using all ones at the start is important. It makes the search grow from every possible source at the same time.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    vector<vector<int>> nearest(vector<vector<int>> grid){
        if (grid.empty() || grid[0].empty()) {
            return grid;
        }

        int rows = grid.size();
        int cols = grid[0].size();
        queue<pair<int, int>> q;
        vector<vector<int>> distance(rows, vector<int>(cols, -1));

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 1) {
                    distance[r][c] = 0;
                    q.push({r, c});
                }
            }
        }

        int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int dc[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!q.empty()) {
            auto [r, c] = q.front();
            q.pop();

            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                int nr = r + dr[k];
                int nc = c + dc[k];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols &&
                    distance[nr][nc] == -1) {
                    distance[nr][nc] = distance[r][c] + 1;
                    q.push({nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
};
```

Time complexity is `O(rows * cols)`. The distance matrix and queue use `O(rows * cols)` extra space.

This uses the fact that a cell can get its answer from the top or left, and later from the bottom or right. Start with a large value for zero cells, then make two sweeps across the grid.

For example, after the first sweep, distances from ones above or to the left are known. The reverse sweep fixes the other directions. A grid with no zero cells stays filled with zeroes.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] nearest(int[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.length == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) {
            return grid;
        }

        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;
        int large = rows + cols + 1;
        int[][] distance = new int[rows][cols];

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 1) {
                    distance[r][c] = 0;
                } else {
                    distance[r][c] = large;
                }
            }
        }

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (r > 0) {
                    distance[r][c] = Math.min(distance[r][c], distance[r - 1][c] + 1);
                }
                if (c > 0) {
                    distance[r][c] = Math.min(distance[r][c], distance[r][c - 1] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        for (int r = rows - 1; r >= 0; r--) {
            for (int c = cols - 1; c >= 0; c--) {
                if (r + 1 < rows) {
                    distance[r][c] = Math.min(distance[r][c], distance[r + 1][c] + 1);
                }
                if (c + 1 < cols) {
                    distance[r][c] = Math.min(distance[r][c], distance[r][c + 1] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return distance;
    }
}
```

The two passes take `O(rows * cols)` time. The result array takes `O(rows * cols)` space. The input grid is not changed.

Here each zero starts its own breadth-first search and stops as soon as a one is reached. That makes the code close to the definition of the task: expand by distance `0`, then `1`, then `2` until a source is found.

This is not the fastest choice for a large grid because nearby cells repeat the same searches. It can still be useful as a simple reference implementation.

```javascript
class Solution {
    nearest(grid) {
        if (!grid || grid.length === 0 || grid[0].length === 0) {
            return grid;
        }

        const rows = grid.length;
        const cols = grid[0].length;
        const result = Array.from({ length: rows }, () => Array(cols).fill(0));
        const directions = [[-1, 0], [1, 0], [0, -1], [0, 1]];

        for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] === 1) {
                    continue;
                }

                const queue = [[r, c, 0]];
                const seen = Array.from({ length: rows }, () => Array(cols).fill(false));
                seen[r][c] = true;
                let head = 0;

                while (head < queue.length) {
                    const [currentR, currentC, distance] = queue[head++];

                    if (grid[currentR][currentC] === 1) {
                        result[r][c] = distance;
                        break;
                    }

                    for (const [dr, dc] of directions) {
                        const nextR = currentR + dr;
                        const nextC = currentC + dc;

                        if (nextR >= 0 && nextR < rows &&
                            nextC >= 0 && nextC < cols &&
                            !seen[nextR][nextC]) {
                            seen[nextR][nextC] = true;
                            queue.push([nextR, nextC, distance + 1]);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The worst-case time is `O((R * C)^2)` and the temporary search data uses `O(R * C)` space. If the grid has no one, zero cells keep the default value, although normal problem inputs contain at least one source.

