## Approach

Since distance here is Manhattan distance with 4-directional movement, each BFS "step" corresponds to exactly one unit of distance this makes multi-source BFS the natural fit instead of running a separate search from every single cell.

- Reverse the search direction: instead of asking "how far is this cell from the nearest 1?" for each cell individually, start simultaneously from all 1-cells and expand outward together.
- Multi-source seeding: push every cell with value 1 into the queue at once, each with dist = 0 they're all equally valid starting points.
- Level-by-level expansion: BFS naturally explores cells in increasing order of distance, so the first time a 0-cell is reached, it's guaranteed to be via the shortest path from some 1.
- Distance propagation: each newly visited cell gets dist = dist[parent] + 1, directly encoding the Manhattan distance to the nearest 1.
- Visited tracking: mark cells as visited the moment they're pushed (not when popped) to avoid redundant pushes and ensure each cell is processed exactly once.

This turns an expensive "one BFS per cell" idea (O((N×M)²)) into a single combined BFS (O(N×M)) by exploiting the fact that all sources can expand together without interfering with each other's correctness.


## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> nearest(vector<vector<int>> grid) {
        int n = grid[0].size(), m = grid.size();  
        vector<vector<int>> dist(m, vector<int>(n, -1));
        vector<vector<bool>> vis(m, vector<bool>(n, false));
        queue<pair<int,int>> q;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    dist[i][j] = 0;
                    vis[i][j] = true;
                    q.push({i, j});
                }
            }
        }

        int dr[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int dc[4] = {0, 0, 1, -1};

        while (!q.empty()) {
            int sz = q.size();

            while (sz--) {
                auto a = q.front();
                q.pop();
                int x = a.first, y = a.second;

                for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                    int ii = x + dr[i];
                    int jj = y + dc[i];

                    if (ii >= 0 && ii < m && jj >= 0 && jj < n && !vis[ii][jj]) {
                        dist[ii][jj] = dist[x][y] + 1;
                        vis[ii][jj] = true;
                        q.push({ii, jj});
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dist;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
Seeding phase: iterate through the entire grid once to find all 1-cells → O(N×M).
BFS phase: every cell is pushed into the queue at most once (guaranteed by the vis[][] check before pushing) and popped exactly once. For each popped cell, you check its 4 neighbors → constant work per cell.
Total BFS work = O(N×M) × O(4) = O(N×M).

